CAT = "dronAirspace"; SEG = "AIR"
Q = [
("What does rule 101.11 require before an unshielded drone enters controlled airspace?",
 "Prior ATC authorisation", ["Call to the local council", "Part 102 certificate", "Report to the police"],
 "Rule 101.11 says a person must not fly an unmanned aircraft in controlled airspace without prior authorisation from the ATC unit responsible for it, unless the operation is shielded."),
("What is the CAA's preferred way to ask for a one-off drone flight in controlled airspace?",
 "Apply through Airshare", ["Email the CAA Director", "Phone the nearest police", "Post a notice at the site"],
 "AC101-1 says the Airshare website is the preferred way to apply for one-off entry to controlled airspace."),
("Before each flight, what must a drone pilot know about the area under rule 101.12?",
 "Its airspace and restrictions", ["Landowner's phone number", "Forecast for next week", "Its local bylaw fines"],
 "Rule 101.12 requires the pilot, before each flight, to be aware of the airspace designation under Part 71 and any airspace restrictions in place where they intend to fly."),
("A child flies a drone without knowing the airspace. How can rule 101.12 still be met?",
 "Supervision by someone who knows", ["Parent's written consent", "Flying under 100 ft only", "Flying for under 5 minutes"],
 "Rule 101.12 allows the flight under the direct supervision of a person who is aware of the airspace designation and any restrictions in the area."),
("Who can give permission to fly a drone inside a restricted area?",
 "Its administering authority", ["Local district council", "Any nearby ATC unit", "Landowner below"],
 "Rule 101.7 says a person must not fly in a restricted area designated under Part 71 without approval from the administering authority responsible for it."),
("Where may a drone never be flown under Part 101, with no permission available?",
 "Low flying zone", ["Danger area", "Restricted area", "Military operating area"],
 "Rule 101.9 bans unmanned aircraft from low flying zones outright. Restricted areas and MOAs can be entered with the administering authority's approval, and danger areas once safety is established."),
("Before flying a drone in a danger area, what must the operator establish?",
 "The activity won't affect safety", ["Who owns the land below", "Area's administering authority", "That ATC has cleared it"],
 "Rule 101.7(c) says an unmanned aircraft must not fly in a danger area unless the person has established that the activity associated with it will not affect the safety of any person or the aircraft."),
("Why does a danger area have no administering authority?",
 "Access needs no control", ["It is always closed to all", "Only drones may use it", "Airways runs it directly"],
 "The CAA airspace guide says danger areas have no administering authority because there is no requirement to control access. A using agency runs the activity, and entry is at the pilot's own risk."),
("What kind of activity is a military operating area set up for?",
 "Military exercises", ["Wildlife breeding colonies", "Airline holding patterns", "Glider training flights"],
 "The CAA airspace guide says an MOA is an area within which military operations, including live firing and explosives, may take place."),
("An MOA on the chart is marked H24. What does that mean?",
 "It is always active", ["It is active from 2400", "It is 24 km wide", "It ends at 2,400 ft"],
 "H24 means permanently active, so unauthorised aircraft, drones included, must stay clear at all times."),
("What do most permanent restricted areas in New Zealand protect?",
 "Wildlife conservation sites", ["Military training ranges", "Airline approach paths", "Hydro dam spillway areas"],
 "The CAA airspace guide says most permanent restricted areas are conservation sites important to New Zealand's wildlife, such as the royal albatross colony at Taiaroa Head."),
("When might a temporary restricted area be set up at short notice?",
 "When Police ask in an emergency", ["For a private wedding party", "Whenever heavy rain falls", "For a local drone race"],
 "Temporary restricted areas can be designated when the Director considers it necessary for safety, security or the public interest, often after a Police request in an emergency."),
("On a visual navigation chart, which letter marks a restricted area?",
 "R", ["M", "D", "L"],
 "Restricted areas are shown on VNCs with an R designator, military operating areas with M, danger areas with D and low flying zones with L."),
("On a chart, what does the designator NZL663 show?",
 "Low flying zone", ["Restricted area", "Danger area", "Volcanic hazard zone"],
 "Airspace designators start with NZ and a letter for the type: L is a low flying zone. The first digit gives the briefing area, so NZL663 is in briefing area 6."),
("What is a low flying zone used for?",
 "Low-level flight training", ["Drone racing events", "Airline arrivals and departures", "Hot air balloon festivals"],
 "The CAA airspace guide says low flying zones allow low-level flight training by a specified using agency, normally a flight training organisation or aero club."),
("From what height to what height does a low flying zone normally extend?",
 "Surface to 500 ft AGL", ["Surface to 400 ft AGL", "500 ft to 1,500 ft AGL", "Surface to 1,000 ft AGL"],
 "Low flying zones normally lie over flat areas and extend from the surface to 500 feet above ground level, which is exactly where drones fly."),
("Where is a mandatory broadcast zone usually set up?",
 "At a busy uncontrolled aerodrome", ["Over a military firing range", "Around a hospital helipad", "Over a city's main shopping area"],
 "The CAA airspace guide says an MBZ is normally established at a busy uncontrolled aerodrome or in airspace with intensive tourist flights. Pilots there broadcast position and intentions on a set frequency."),
("A drone pilot plans to fly inside a mandatory broadcast zone. What does the zone tell them about the area?",
 "Manned traffic is busy there", ["Drones are banned there", "ATC controls all traffic there", "Only gliders fly there"],
 "An MBZ is set up where uncontrolled traffic is busy, so manned aircraft are likely nearby. A drone pilot must still give way to them and keep the 4 km aerodrome rules."),
("What is the purpose of a volcanic hazard zone?",
 "To warn of volcanic activity", ["To protect a wildlife colony", "To hold military exercises", "To train new pilots"],
 "A volcanic hazard zone is designated where volcanic activity may be present. New Zealand's most active volcanoes, such as Ruapehu and Whakaari White Island, have permanent zones."),
("Which airspace classes are used in New Zealand?",
 "A, C, D and G", ["A, B, C and D", "B, C, E and G", "C, D, E and F"],
 "The CAA airspace guide says New Zealand uses ICAO classes A, C, D and G. Classes B, E and F are not currently used."),
("Which kind of controlled airspace starts at the surface?",
 "Control zone (CTR)", ["Control area (CTA)", "Oceanic control area", "VFR transit lane"],
 "Control zones protect arriving and departing IFR flights and are the only controlled airspace that touches the surface. Control areas sit above a set lower limit."),
("Which class of airspace is uncontrolled?",
 "Class G", ["Class C", "Class D", "Class A"],
 "Class G is uncontrolled airspace. Classes A, C and D are controlled, and entry to C and D needs an ATC clearance."),
("A busy international airport is ringed by which class of control zone?",
 "Class C", ["Class D", "Class G", "Class A"],
 "The CAA airspace guide says Class C applies to control zones at large international aerodromes and their control areas. Class D normally applies around regional aerodromes."),
("Where are temporary airspace restrictions found that a visual navigation chart does not show?",
 "NOTAMs and AIP supplements", ["Drone's user manual", "Council's rates notice", "Part 102 exposition"],
 "AC101-1 says VNCs do not show temporary airspace, so pilots must check NOTAMs and AIP supplements on AIP New Zealand."),
("What is a NOTAM?",
 "Notice of a hazard or change", ["Drone registration number", "Type of pilot licence", "Permanent airspace map"],
 "NOTAMs are notices issued about changes to airspace, aerodromes or hazards that need immediate notice to pilots."),
("Where can the coordinates of New Zealand's temporary and permanent airspace be looked up free of charge?",
 "Air Navigation Register", ["Part 47 aircraft register", "Companies Office register", "Council's district plan"],
 "The Air Navigation Register on the AIP New Zealand website holds the geographic coordinates of temporary and permanent airspace, free of charge."),
("An MOA overlaps controlled airspace. Whose requirements must a pilot meet first?",
 "MOA's", ["Controlled airspace's", "Neither, they cancel out", "Whichever is less strict"],
 "The CAA airspace guide says special use airspace requirements take precedence over the class of airspace where they overlap, so the MOA's requirements are met first."),
("Does a low flying zone change the class of the airspace it sits in?",
 "No, the class stays", ["Yes, it becomes Class G", "Yes, it becomes Class D", "Yes, it becomes controlled"],
 "Special use airspace does not change the underlying class: an LFZ inside a Class D control zone stays Class D, and one outside stays Class G."),
("How low may military aircraft fly outside controlled airspace and other set areas?",
 "Down to 250 ft AGL", ["Down to 500 ft AGL", "Down to 1,000 ft AGL", "Never below 400 ft AGL"],
 "The CAA airspace guide says military low-level flying down to 250 feet above ground level may happen at any time outside controlled airspace, aerodromes, restricted areas and LFZs, clear of built-up areas."),
("Does an Airshare approval to enter controlled airspace mean the whole flight is legal?",
 "No, other rules still apply", ["Yes, for the whole day", "Yes, if it stays under 400 ft", "Yes, if it is shielded"],
 "AC101-1 says Airshare approval covers the airspace only. Every other Part 101 rule still applies, so the pilot may need other permissions such as consent from people and property owners."),
("Inside a Class C or Class D control zone, which unmanned flight needs no ATC authorisation?",
 "Fully shielded operation", ["Any flight below 400 ft", "Any flight under 2 kg", "Any flight over private land"],
 "Rule 101.11 exempts shielded operations, and AC101-1 says flights in controlled airspace may skip ATC authorisation if the entire operation is shielded."),
("A volcanic hazard zone is raised to a higher alert level. How is that usually made known?",
 "By NOTAM", ["By text from Airways", "By a sign at the summit", "By the drone's app only"],
 "The CAA airspace guide says VHZs grow in size as the alert level rises, and the change is advised by NOTAM, with ash clouds advised by SIGMET."),
]
